# C and C++ Safety

- Assign one owner to every allocation, descriptor, mapping, lock, and thread.
- Check allocation, arithmetic, conversion, indexing, I/O, and syscall results.
- Prove pointer arithmetic remains within one valid object and lifetime.
- Handle partial initialization, short I/O, `EINTR`, timeout, and every cleanup
  path without double release.
- Prefer RAII and value semantics in C++; use one auditable cleanup path in C.
- Reject unbounded C string operations, signed-size confusion, integer overflow,
  use-after-free, strict-aliasing violations, and data races.
- Compile changed code with strict warnings; use sanitizers or Valgrind when
  execution is safe and relevant.
